  "_exec_summary": [
    {
      "ESSeqNum": 0,
      "FacilityID": 1000123373,
      "SummaryText": "1.\tAccidental release prevention and emergency response policies\n\nOur company is committed to health, safety, and environmental excellence as stated in our Corporate Environmental, Health, Safety & Security Policy. To accomplish this, we integrate health, safety, security and environmental planning into all business activities. This includes complying with applicable laws, responsibly managing risks, and working collaboratively with others in addressing health, safety, security and environmental issues.  Our company maintains corporate Environmental, Health and Safety Standards that define requirements and guidelines developed to prevent injuries and accidental releases.\n\nOur company has corporate programs on release prevention and emergency preparedness standards to protect people and the environment.  Our corporate Emergency Preparedness and Response standard requires site specific Emergency Response Plan to include procedures for managing releases.\n\n2.\tStationary source and regulated substances handled\n\nThe La Grande site manufactures formaldehyde solutions and urea-formaldehyde resins. Formaldehyde is primarily used on site as a raw material for urea-formaldehyde resin manufacturing. \n\nThe regulated substance used in the plant and the maximum quantities stored are:\nFormaldehyde: 624,000 lbs.\n\n3.\tAccidental release prevention program and chemical-specific prevention steps\n\nThe plant has a comprehensive process safety management program that follows the EPA Accidental Release Prevention Rule, the OSHA Process Safety Management Standard, and all applicable state regulations.  An electronic management system is in place to ensure on-going compliance. This management system includes identified safety related tasks assigned to associates with a required completion date. The Site Leader has overall responsibility for this program. \n\nDesignated personnel are assigned to maintain and improve the safety of their units. Their duties include keeping operating procedures up-"
    },
    {
      "ESSeqNum": 1,
      "FacilityID": 1000123373,
      "SummaryText": "to-date, training operators so they can safely carry out these procedures, assuring that the plant is run safely on a daily basis, and when necessary, investigating incidents in their unit. All employees participate in the safety program to improve safety and plant operation. \n\nDesignated personnel also maintain the Operating Guide that includes process safety information, operating procedures, and a training certification program. Operators use this Operating Guide as a training tool and for reference. Their actions will prevent accidental releases by applying the information it contains.\n\nThe site conducts process hazard analyses on an on-going basis to identify hazards and recommend safeguards that will prevent an accidental release. Recommendations resulting from the process hazard analyses are entered into an action items database to track the items to completion.\n\nA Management of Change (MOC) system assures that changes to plant equipment, procedures, or processes do not cause accidents. MOC initiates a safety review for equipment, procedure, and process changes, and may include a process hazard analysis. Changes to product formulations initiate a review by technical and plant personnel.\n\nA preventive maintenance program maintains the mechanical integrity of process equipment. A computer database is used to manage the preventive maintenance schedule and other corrective or improvement repairs necessary to maintain the ongoing integrity and reliability of the facility equipment. Each month scheduled equipment inspections, tests, or service are performed. The schedule is based on plant experience to reduce the likelihood of an accidental release caused by equipment failure.\n\nThe requirements in the corporate standards include safe work practices which prevent accidental releases.  There is a corporate standard for hot work that utilizes a permit system to reduce the risk of fire. There is also a corporate stand for lockout and tagout of equipment for maintenance"
    },
    {
      "ESSeqNum": 2,
      "FacilityID": 1000123373,
      "SummaryText": ". This standard reduces the likelihood that a valve position or operation error will lead to a release.\n\nContractors who periodically perform work in the plant are given safety orientations to brief them on plant hazards and safety practices. Contractor safety programs and performance are evaluated prior to their selection for jobs that impact process safety.\nIncidents that cause or could have caused a release are investigated and recommendations are made to prevent recurrence. These recommendations may lead to improvements in equipment, procedures, operating conditions, or training.\n\nInternal Corporate Health, Safety and Environmental Audits are conducted periodically to maintain compliance with local, state and federal regulations. Addressing recommendations from these audits and reviews keeps the Facility's Safety and Environmental programs effective.\n\n4.\tFive-year accident history\n\nIn the five-year period from September 30, 2020 to September 19, 2025 there were no reportable off-site releases of regulated substances.\n\n5.\tEmergency response program\n\nThe site Emergency Response Plan describes procedures for identified potential release scenarios, including coordination with fire department responders. The site conducts an emergency response drill annually to test the Emergency Response Plan and reinforce training that plant responders receive.\n\n6. Planned changes to improve safety\n\nNo significant changes are planned."
    }
  ]
